WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 12 noted that ethics is not the same as legality and left the distinction here. Chapter 7 built the controls and Chapter 12 observed that each of them depends on people being willing to do their jobs honestly. Chapter 1 defined political and compliance risk, which are what a values conflict looks like when it reaches the risk register.
15.1  Applying values across borders
The outcome asks for the issues an organisation faces in applying its values and ethical standards internationally, and there are seven worth naming.
Different laws, and conflicting ones. Employment protections, environmental limits, data rules, disclosure requirements and competition law differ, and occasionally a company is required by one country's law to do what another's forbids. Data transfer and sanctions produce the sharpest cases.
Different customs around gifts, hospitality and payments. In some markets a payment to an official to do what they are already obliged to do — clear a shipment, issue a licence — is routine and expected. It is a facilitation payment, it is a criminal offence under the laws Chapter 18 describes, and the company that refuses it bears a real cost: a consignment held five days costs Orontes 46,027 × 5 = 230,135 of contribution, against a payment of 2,000 which is 2,000 ÷ 450,000 = 0.44 per cent of the shipment's value. The arithmetic is exactly why the rule has to be absolute: anything less than a prohibition becomes a calculation, and the calculation always favours paying.
Different labour and safety standards. Working hours, minimum ages, union rights, accommodation and safety equipment. A company may be entirely lawful locally and far below what it would accept at home, and the question is which standard applies to its own operations and to its suppliers'.
Different environmental standards, with the same structure.
Conduct in the supply chain and among partners. A company is judged by what is done in its name — by its distributors, agents, joint-venture partners and suppliers — and it usually has less control over them than over its own staff. Agents in particular are the standard route by which bribery enters a company that has prohibited it.
The reach of home-country law. Anti-bribery statutes, sanctions and some tax and disclosure rules apply to conduct abroad, so "it is legal there" is not even a legal defence, let alone an ethical one.
And the practical problems of applying a code at a distance: translation that must carry meaning and not only words, local managers who have to interpret general principles in situations the code never contemplated, training delivered in a language and a format that work locally, monitoring that is genuine rather than a questionnaire, and a reporting route a local employee will actually use.
