15.2  Three positions, and the workable one
Two extreme positions are available and both fail, which is why the exam wants the middle one described.
Absolutism holds that the company's home standards apply everywhere without variation. Its strength is clarity: nobody has to interpret anything, and the rule is the same in every office. Its weaknesses are that it can be arrogant — not every local practice is worse, merely different — that it may be impractical or even unlawful locally, and that it leaves no room for the genuine differences in custom that have nothing to do with right and wrong.
Relativism holds that the right standard is whatever is accepted locally — "when in Rome". Its strength is respect for local practice and the recognition that outsiders understand less than they think. Its weakness is fatal: it provides no ground on which to refuse anything, since every practice is accepted somewhere, and it hands the standard to whoever benefits from the worst local custom.
The workable position distinguishes between core values and local practice.
Core values do not vary. Honesty, safety, no bribery, no forced or child labour, no deliberate misstatement, no discrimination of the kinds the company has named: these apply in every country and in every operation, and they are the zero-tolerance categories of Chapter 6's appetite statement.
Local practice varies, and should. Negotiating style, the role of relationships and intermediaries, how directly disagreement is expressed, hospitality norms, hierarchy, working patterns, how decisions are made and recorded. A company that treats these as ethical matters mistakes unfamiliarity for wrongdoing.
The hard cases live in between, and the exam uses them: a gift that is customary and substantial; a hiring practice that favours family and is universal locally; a supplier whose working conditions are lawful and poor; a payment that is demanded rather than offered. Four tests help.
Would we do it at home, and if not, what exactly is different? Sometimes the answer is a real difference in circumstance and sometimes it is only that nobody would see.
Is it lawful under every law that reaches us, including the home-country law of section 15.1?
Could we describe it publicly — to our own employees, to a customer, to a newspaper?
And is it being required of us, or offered to us? Extortion and opportunity are different cases, and a payment demanded under threat of harm is treated differently in most anti-bribery regimes from one offered to win business — though the company should still report and record it.
