15.3  Ethical and legal behaviour
The outcome asks for the differences, and four of them matter.
Law is a floor; ethics is a standard. Everything the law forbids is unethical in the ordinary case, but a great deal that is lawful is not ethical: a contract term enforced against a party who did not understand it, a redundancy handled lawfully and brutally, a marketing claim technically true and designed to mislead, a tax structure that is legal and indefensible in public. Legal compliance is the minimum, not the objective.
Law is enforced externally; ethics is enforced internally and professionally. The sanction for illegality is a court; the sanction for unethical conduct is reputation, professional membership, trust and the loss of people who will not work for the company.
Law is specific, codified and backward-looking. It names acts, in writing, after a problem has been recognised. Ethics applies to situations no rule has contemplated — new technologies, new markets, new products — and it operates before the law catches up. The data ethics of Chapter 18 is the clearest current example.
And law varies by jurisdiction; core ethical values are held to be the same everywhere, which is section 15.2's point.
Figure F815-02 sets out the four combinations.
Figure F815-02. Legal and ethical
@table
Case | Example | What to do
Legal and ethical | Ordinary trading on agreed terms | Proceed
Legal but unethical | A technically true marketing claim designed to mislead | Refuse; the law is not the standard
Illegal but widely tolerated locally | A facilitation payment | Refuse; and report the demand
Illegal and unethical | Bribery to win a contract | Refuse; report; investigate
@end
Two further points the examiners use.
A legal requirement can conflict with an ethical obligation. A local law requiring disclosure of information a professional holds in confidence, or forbidding the employment of a group the company's values require it to treat equally, puts the two in direct conflict. The resolution is legal advice, escalation and, where the conflict is irreducible, a decision about whether to operate there at all.
And "it is not illegal" is the most reliable warning phrase in business. It is almost never offered about a decision that is comfortable, and a professional who hears it should ask what the speaker thinks the ethical objection is, since they have evidently thought of one.
