15.4  Ethics and internal control
The third outcome asks for the relationship between ethics and internal control, and it runs in both directions.
Ethics is the foundation of internal control. COSO's internal control framework puts the control environment first among its five components and makes a commitment to integrity and ethical values its first principle, for a reason: every control is performed by somebody, and a control performed by somebody willing to subvert it is not a control. Chapter 12 made the point with the table of frauds, and three consequences follow.
Controls can be overridden. Management override is the most common way serious control failures occur and the hardest to prevent, because the person overriding has the authority to do so. No amount of control design addresses it; what addresses it is a culture in which an override is questioned and a route by which the question reaches somebody independent.
Controls can be circumvented by collusion. Segregation of duties works because two people must agree, and it fails when they do. The defence is ethical and cultural as much as procedural.
And controls that are not believed in are not performed. A reconciliation signed without being done, an approval given without being considered, a supplier review completed from memory: these are the ordinary way a control framework hollows out, and nothing in the documentation reveals it.
Internal control supports ethical behaviour. The relationship runs back the other way too, and this half is as examinable.
Controls remove opportunity, which is one side of Chapter 14's triangle, and they remove it from honest people as well as dishonest ones. Segregation of duties protects the person who handles cash from suspicion as much as it protects the cash.
Controls make the standard concrete. A code that says "we do not accept inappropriate gifts" is interpreted; a control that requires every gift above a value to be recorded in a register that somebody reviews is not.
Controls create the record on which accountability depends — Chapter 12's duty to account.
And controls reduce the pressure on individual courage. A professional who can point to a rule, a threshold or a required approval is in a far stronger position than one who must refuse on personal judgment alone, and that is one of the most practical arguments for having written policies at all.
The two together produce the conclusion the exam wants: neither ethics nor control is sufficient alone. Controls without ethics are a structure people work around; ethics without controls is a good intention with no record, no consistency and no protection for the person who has to say no.
