LANGUAGE FOCUS   "When in Rome", "grease" and "gift"
Three English expressions do a great deal of quiet work in this area. "When in Rome, do as the Romans do" is relativism compressed into a proverb, and it is almost always produced to justify something the speaker would not do at home — which is the test that defeats it. Grease payment is the informal term for a facilitation payment, and the metaphor is doing the arguing: grease makes machinery run smoothly and sounds harmless, which is the opposite of what the laws of Chapter 18 say about it. And gift is the most elastic word of all, covering a calendar, a dinner, a holiday and a payment; English practice in codes of conduct is therefore to abandon the word and specify — anything of value, including hospitality, travel, services and offers of employment to relatives. Arabic هدية and French cadeau are equally elastic, and the same solution applies in both. When writing or reading a code, look for the specifics; where a code says only "inappropriate gifts", nobody has yet decided anything.
