Chapter summary
Figure F815-04 brings the chapter together.
Figure F815-04. Chapter 15 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Applying values internationally raises seven issues: conflicting laws, customs around gifts and facilitation payments, labour and safety standards, environmental standards, conduct by agents and suppliers, the extraterritorial reach of home-country law, and the practical difficulty of translating, interpreting, training and monitoring a code at a distance. Absolutism is clear and can be arrogant or unlawful; relativism respects local practice and provides no ground to refuse anything; the workable position holds core values constant — honesty, safety, no bribery, no forced or child labour, no deliberate misstatement — while allowing local practice to vary, and tests the hard cases by whether it would be done at home, whether every applicable law permits it, whether it could be described publicly, and whether it is demanded or offered. Law is a floor and ethics a standard; law is enforced externally and ethics professionally and reputationally; law is codified and backward-looking while ethics reaches situations no rule anticipated; and law varies by jurisdiction where core values do not. Ethics is the foundation of internal control, because controls can be overridden, circumvented by collusion or hollowed out by people who do not believe in them; and internal control supports ethics, by removing opportunity, making the standard concrete, creating the record accountability depends on, and relieving individuals of having to refuse on personal judgment alone — so neither is sufficient without the other. And designing for both means a specific code that covers agents, controls adapted to the local situation rather than documented fictions, third-party due diligence with rights to audit and terminate, a reporting route people will actually use, situational training, and a recognition that a five-day delay costing 46,027 × 5 = 230,135 must be borne by somebody who is not then penalised for bearing it.
