Case-style set: the new distributor's request
What is being asked. Four features of the request point the same way: a payment of 4 per cent of 2,400,000, which is 96,000 a year, to a party Orontes has not chosen; in cash; in a third country; with no written agreement and no defined deliverable. Each of those is a recognised indicator of a corrupt arrangement, and together they are close to a complete set. The stated services — registration and shelf placement — are the kind of thing that may be legitimate consultancy or may be a channel to officials, and the absence of any contract means the company could not tell which afterwards.
Why the local manager's argument does not settle it. "The market cannot be entered without it" may be entirely true, and it is not an answer. If entering a market requires an arrangement the company cannot describe publicly and cannot document, the correct conclusion is that this market cannot be entered on these terms — which is Chapter 5's avoidance response, chosen because the exposure cannot be brought within appetite by any other means.
What I would do. Decline the arrangement as presented, in writing, with the reason stated as a matter of company policy rather than as an accusation against the distributor. Ask for the alternative: a named consultancy with a written contract, a defined scope, invoices against deliverables, payment by bank transfer to an account in the country where the services are performed, and the right to audit. If the distributor will accept that, the arrangement may well be legitimate and the problem was its form. If it will not, that refusal is the answer to the question.
What the company should put in place for this market. Third-party due diligence before appointment, covering ownership, political connections and reputation, with the results documented. Contractual clauses requiring compliance with anti-bribery laws, giving a right to audit and a right to terminate on breach. Payment terms that make cash and third-country payments impossible. Training for the local manager in situations rather than rules, and a reporting route in the local language. And — the part most often omitted — a commercial plan that does not depend on the arrangement being refused, so that the manager who refuses it is not the person who misses the target.
